WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:07.230
[ANITA]: Hello and welcome!

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:13.330
I'm Anita Wood, the program manager for the
Stanford energy innovation and emerging technologies

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:18.320
program, also known as the EIET program.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:22.940
On behalf of the Stanford Center for Professional
Development and the Stanford Bits and Watts

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:27.400
Initiative, which is in the Stanford Precourt
Institute for energy, I would like to welcome

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:34.230
you to this special session on the transformation
of the electric grid with Dr. Ram Rajagopal.

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:39.960
Ram is an associate professor of civil and
environmental engineering and a senior fellow

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:42.570
at the Stanford Precourt Institute for Energy.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:52.120
Dr. Rajagopal teaches the EIET237: Transforming
the Grid course in the Stanford EIET program.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:57.719
He directs the Stanford Sustainable Systems
lab, which is focused on large-scale monitoring,

00:00:57.719 --> 00:01:03.359
data analytics, and stochastic control for
infrastructure networks, in particular power

00:01:03.359 --> 00:01:08.380
networks, and his current research interests
in power systems are in the integration of

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:13.730
renewables, smart distribution systems, and
demand side data analytics.

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:19.450
For this session, he will be talking about
looking beyond technical considerations and

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:21.970
integrating distributed energy resources.

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:27.290
He will also talk about cyber constraints,
privacy, and energy equity.

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:33.290
Ram's presentation will be followed by several
pre-recorded questions and answers.

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:34.290
Now let's begin.

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:40.540
[RAM]: First of all thank you for hosting
our session just here on the left hand side

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:48.460
of this very first slide, I chose a picture
of something that is hot off our research

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:50.720
to show the power of open data.

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:56.790
We have been able to analyze the demand -- hourly
demand -- from 58 countries around the world

00:01:56.790 --> 00:02:04.140
to understand the impact of COVID-19 restrictions
on the demand and you can see here this work

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:07.810
was entirely done by a group of students and
postdocs.

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:13.900
I thought a little bit about what is interesting
today and kind of the title of the presentation

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:18.629
reflects that at Stanford, what is our, some
of our key interests.

00:02:18.629 --> 00:02:26.519
These are the concepts I have here: virtualization,
learning coordination, and privacy/fairness

00:02:26.519 --> 00:02:29.939
in these consumer driven distributed energy
systems.

00:02:29.939 --> 00:02:37.569
This is very important because the word consumer
here means there is people and their preferences

00:02:37.569 --> 00:02:43.950
that need to be taken into account, so I think
that's a major distinction of the traditional

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:46.090
view of such systems.

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:49.999
You know we all are familiar with the grid
I'm just going to go very quickly here and

00:02:49.999 --> 00:02:55.909
the traditional grid is changing because we
have massive adoption of renewables and new

00:02:55.909 --> 00:03:02.360
technologies such as storage at the transmission
level, but at the same time behind the meter

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:09.290
consumers are adopting new technologies as
well, like EV charging, smart appliances,

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:15.319
self-generation through solar PV for example,
and all of these technologies are connected

00:03:15.319 --> 00:03:17.060
to the cloud.

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:26.370
This enables us to collect data and send signals
and potentially coordinate all these resources

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:33.200
to achieve individual and joint goals that
can serve, you know, to the wholesale market

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:39.870
so you can have flexible load that follows
generation instead of the other way around,

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:47.310
but also as we start to see of increased adoption
of these technologies, this coordination can

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:50.670
be used to mitigate the impacts on the distribution
network.

00:03:50.670 --> 00:04:02.920
So, one of the research focuses here in Stanford
has really been around how do we utilize this

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:09.810
capability behind the meter to support the
rest of the grid ecosystem, and when we say

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:13.419
that what are the key challenges here?

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:20.860
The first one is that we need to understand
what is the real flexibility available at

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:26.380
each customer and that critically depends
not just on the technologies they have but

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:31.440
also their preferences: how much are they
willing to change, adapt, and adjust, and

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:36.460
learning that at the scale that needs to be
done is something that still remains extremely

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:43.600
challenging and an open question and something
that I feel for example you need not just

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:48.910
methods, but a lot of open data interaction
with customers and so on.

00:04:48.910 --> 00:04:53.611
The second question is, well, if we know the
consumer preferences and assuming we have

00:04:53.611 --> 00:05:00.590
a model for the distribution network, then
we can start to think about how to scale this

00:05:00.590 --> 00:05:04.290
coordination to millions of heterogeneous
resources.

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:09.970
Again the key word here is scaling there is
many many strategies and architectures for

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:14.950
coordination; this is very well known, but
do they actually scale to the scale we need

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:15.950
them to go?

00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:24.820
The third one I think is very critical and
goes into the economics and policy because

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:31.570
it is all about whatever strategies you create,
whatever platforms, ideas, etc, be it to collect

00:05:31.570 --> 00:05:38.210
data or to coordinate, you definitely need
to make it easy and attractive for consumers

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:39.520
to engage.

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:44.810
That means not asking them tons of questions,
you know, or are asking a lot of effort on

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:49.660
their part, and also mechanisms to compensate
them.

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:55.760
Last but not least I think we're seeing a
dramatic change in society today regarding

00:05:55.760 --> 00:06:03.140
two ideas and I think it's time we took them
more deeply into how we design these energy

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:05.681
systems, particularly consumer driven ones.

00:06:05.681 --> 00:06:11.820
One is this notion of privacy, we are very
familiar with this, there was some early work

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:19.000
on this looking at privacy of smart meter
data and so on, but in those days, the entity

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:26.810
that took care of the data was just the utility.
So the privacy was in a way managed by utility

00:06:26.810 --> 00:06:33.690
regulators, but now we're talking about aggregators,
different types of service providers, and so

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:42.270
on; we need a broader and expanded definition
of what privacy is, and actually ways to enforce

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:49.520
it, monitor it, measure it. And the second topic
I think is one of fairness, so I will address

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:55.060
those briefly at the end of the presentation.
What we mean by fairness is we need to give

00:06:55.060 --> 00:07:04.410
both equal access to the participation in
these modern systems, but we also need ways

00:07:04.410 --> 00:07:11.140
to ensure that the systems we create don't
exploit particularly vulnerable customers,

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:16.340
so you know, families who have lots of people
who stay home tend to be lower income, and

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:22.120
maybe your demand response algorithm would
continuously target them for turning on and

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:27.470
off. These are considerations that have been
taken into account in economics and more now

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:33.140
on web search and things like that, but I think
there's a whole open question about how to

00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:40.360
do this from a technical perspective, but also
institutionally how utilities and companies,

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:46.300
aggregators, technology providers, policy makers
are going to deal with these issues. I just

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:51.900
wanted to kick off the conversation with something
that we did recently which is kind of unusual

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:59.870
here for our group. We worked with a sociologist
and a psychologist to try to understand in

00:07:59.870 --> 00:08:08.870
a real population how much actual consumer
flexibility there is on their loads and devices

00:08:08.870 --> 00:08:15.560
that you see. And in this graph here, what you
see is really what percentage of each one

00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:23.210
of these activities is performed during the
peak time. This was a population of 370 individuals,

00:08:23.210 --> 00:08:28.430
and the paper came out in energy policy around
this and you can see here for example TV is

00:08:28.430 --> 00:08:35.760
watched 90 during the peak time, etc. And in
black, that's kind of the willingness to shift

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:41.829
what percentage of people were willing to
shift, and you can see that just very few items

00:08:41.829 --> 00:08:49.810
like your washer, dryer, dishwasher are things
that both meet somewhat being used in the

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:55.420
peak time, and somewhat willing to shift. So
this started shaping a lot of our groups thinking

00:08:55.420 --> 00:09:00.930
on how to design the systems that respond
to this need, and I'll address that when we

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:06.089
talk a little bit about powernet, but I think
this kind of engagement and interaction with

00:09:06.089 --> 00:09:11.839
consumers now they're doing a project where
we educate these households through their

00:09:11.839 --> 00:09:18.279
kids to understand their own consumption from
smart meter data and then try to execute on

00:09:18.279 --> 00:09:22.570
the shift. It's a little bit away from the
traditional path but it's all about how do

00:09:22.570 --> 00:09:28.730
you have this human in the loop during this
kind of management, and it's a ton of work

00:09:28.730 --> 00:09:34.329
and very interesting as well, so if you come
here in the summer, we have workshops with

00:09:34.329 --> 00:09:42.379
you know, hundreds of kids and it's super exciting.
Okay, going more towards learning, you know

00:09:42.379 --> 00:09:48.709
the first idea I feel is very critical is
that in economics, there's a gold standard

00:09:48.709 --> 00:09:54.749
about how you learn preferences. You usually
say people have a utility function, I'm going

00:09:54.749 --> 00:10:00.561
to show them different choices, observe the
choice they make, and then model this utility

00:10:00.561 --> 00:10:06.029
function that is kind of the embedded value
of the choice. And there is lots of techniques

00:10:06.029 --> 00:10:12.339
for that, and it's well known. This typically
translates in the utility world of electric

00:10:12.339 --> 00:10:19.540
utilities, as we will do randomized trials
on different programs that we have, let's say

00:10:19.540 --> 00:10:25.149
for demand response, to understand the size
of the effect, and then model the heterogeneity

00:10:25.149 --> 00:10:32.209
of that effect as a function of customer characteristics
collected from questionnaires. It turns out

00:10:32.209 --> 00:10:39.009
that these models in the electricity side
perform not so great. Typically if you use

00:10:39.009 --> 00:10:45.000
created models like this and we work closely
for example with PG&amp;E on looking at this and

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:50.970
then you say, now I want to recruit enough
customers to have a megawatt of demand response

00:10:50.970 --> 00:10:55.589
on this particular feeder, you end up with
something like 10 to 20 percent of that. That's

00:10:55.589 --> 00:11:00.019
kind of it, called the yield. Just as a point
of comparison you know if you go and talk

00:11:00.019 --> 00:11:06.520
to somebody at amazon, they will say that these
offers that are put on your first landing

00:11:06.520 --> 00:11:12.689
page of the amazon website when you see it,
75 of the people click on one of those offers

00:11:12.689 --> 00:11:17.170
and then of those clicks, between 50 to 70
percent of the people actually purchased what

00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:23.050
they saw. So that is overall much more than
like a demand response offer where you're

00:11:23.050 --> 00:11:27.509
actually offering money to the customer, and
yet the engagement is less. Economists looked

00:11:27.509 --> 00:11:31.730
at these questions in the last 10 years and
there's a really beautiful stream of literature

00:11:31.730 --> 00:11:38.360
on that, and what they kind of found is that
this is called the issue with stated preferences

00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:43.290
because normally, we are not able to observe
what actions customers take with regards to

00:11:43.290 --> 00:11:49.839
their electricity consumption. So you do polls
and surveys like the one I had before, and

00:11:49.839 --> 00:11:56.449
then use those polls and surveys to predict
how much flexibility customers have, and that

00:11:56.449 --> 00:12:03.199
goes directly into the planning. And so those
predictions don't completely capture the preferences

00:12:03.199 --> 00:12:07.860
because customers don't pay a lot of attention
to their electricity bill; it's not a big part

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:12.740
of their budget. So this is an issue not just
with electricity in many other areas declared

00:12:12.740 --> 00:12:18.240
preferences of things that won't don't weigh
on your pocket as much are not accurate. So

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:25.779
one idea that we developed in partnership
with PG&amp;E, ESE, and a host of utilities was this

00:12:25.779 --> 00:12:32.850
idea that, well I have a smart meter that's
like high resolution temporal data. That data,

00:12:32.850 --> 00:12:39.889
it's like an MRI of customers preferences.
If I could interpret that MRI, and by that,

00:12:39.889 --> 00:12:45.089
here is what I mean, extract features like
what's the peak load, base load, what's the

00:12:45.089 --> 00:12:51.149
thermal sensitivity, what are the load shapes,
I can use these features to build these models

00:12:51.149 --> 00:12:56.450
for segmentation, targeting, etc. This gave rise to
a project called Wisdom which was carried

00:12:56.450 --> 00:13:04.300
out here at Stanford from 2011 to 2016, and
then we released this open source package

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:09.550
called Wisdom it's now used in eight U.S utilities
in Southern California, Edison PG&amp;E, Vermont,

00:13:09.550 --> 00:13:18.480
etc, and then a few more worldwide. And it's
completely free, open source, you can download,

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:26.500
and i think in 2016 when we released it, we
had analyzed about 8 million smart meters

00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:31.350
with this package. Since then you know we haven't
really kept track of what's going on, so

00:13:31.350 --> 00:13:37.720
just as an initial, like, the very first result
from Wisdom is shown here. We just did the

00:13:37.720 --> 00:13:44.450
first study ever at the time at the scale
that was done, which was 66 million customer

00:13:44.450 --> 00:13:50.990
days. We looked at 66 million customer days
across the bay area and performed clustering.

00:13:50.990 --> 00:13:56.910
And when we started doing this study, PG&amp;E
gave us the data that took itself like a year

00:13:56.910 --> 00:14:03.029
or two, and then when we did the study initially
the consensus was, well why should we do this,

00:14:03.029 --> 00:14:09.769
it's well known in planning that all customers
are this dual peak shape. And then when we

00:14:09.769 --> 00:14:15.939
actually did the clustering, we found many
different load shapes for each day, including

00:14:15.939 --> 00:14:24.579
just consuming at night, very flat consumption,
evening peaks, and the so-called planning option.

00:14:24.579 --> 00:14:29.490
It's only fourteen percent of the load shapes.
And this is very critical because, for example,

00:14:29.490 --> 00:14:36.760
load shape number 811 here actually can have
demand response capability at 3 pm, versus

00:14:36.760 --> 00:14:45.449
this guy here, 14, can have no such significant
capability. So first lesson from this project

00:14:45.449 --> 00:14:53.300
is when you're thinking about these new kinds
of flexibility that are emerging, number one,

00:14:53.300 --> 00:14:59.279
due to the preferences of consumers, the flexibility
may or may not be there, like you saw from

00:14:59.279 --> 00:15:07.490
the survey. Number two, data and massive data
are essential to have accurate models of consumer

00:15:07.490 --> 00:15:13.029
preferences to achieve the types of performance
we need to make these things cost effective

00:15:13.029 --> 00:15:19.689
for the future. So the second question we had
in our list up there was, okay so we understand

00:15:19.689 --> 00:15:26.600
customer preferences, maybe we can collect
a few of these consumers, and use their capabilities

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:32.699
to offer services back to the grid, or to reduce
their bill, or something like that. How do we

00:15:32.699 --> 00:15:38.490
do that at scale? That was the genesis of this
project Powernet. It's a project that has been

00:15:38.490 --> 00:15:45.350
funded by the California Energy Commission
and ARPA-E as part of the nodes program, and

00:15:45.350 --> 00:15:51.129
we had as partners Zodian, Suntech drive and
Google. Google also funded the project and

00:15:51.129 --> 00:15:56.390
it's also a partner of the technology. And
it started out with a simple, kind of interesting

00:15:56.390 --> 00:16:04.779
observation. At the time we started this project
in my group and in Abbas El Gamal's, he's another

00:16:04.779 --> 00:16:10.579
faculty here at Stanford, in his group we had
had a few collaborations around optimization

00:16:10.579 --> 00:16:17.920
algorithms for storage and kind of distributed
control, and this is kind of a big fad in power

00:16:17.920 --> 00:16:26.490
systems. And these algorithms work great in
Matlab, and that means in simulation, and then

00:16:26.490 --> 00:16:34.279
you know, I visited Google and Arun Majumdar's
former group, the one that he created because

00:16:34.279 --> 00:16:41.879
I had this kind of question that was put forward
by my student Gustavo, well, but how do we know

00:16:41.879 --> 00:16:46.509
these algorithms actually work in the real
world? And then somebody told me Arun was doing

00:16:46.509 --> 00:16:51.489
this in Google, and we went and visited that
group. And what they had tried was actually

00:16:51.489 --> 00:16:58.769
to implement in a lab scale with realistic
constraints in terms of the cyber constraints,

00:16:58.769 --> 00:17:04.380
the information exchange constraints, several
of these algorithmic strategies. And what they

00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:12.269
found out is that fully distributed algorithms,
although very elegant and beautiful theoretically,

00:17:12.269 --> 00:17:16.970
they don't really scale well, and it's very
hard to diagnose, it's very expensive to try

00:17:16.970 --> 00:17:23.510
to set them up with customers, and so on. And
in fact at that time, they had just managed

00:17:23.510 --> 00:17:29.419
to do like one home, and technologies inside
it and tried to coordinate the energy consumption.

00:17:29.419 --> 00:17:34.200
And they had done a survey with customers
because they needed to collect preferences,

00:17:34.200 --> 00:17:40.809
and Google does these user surveys, and they
said well, we're going to collect your preferences,

00:17:40.809 --> 00:17:46.020
and here's our idea. We're going to put forward
a questionnaire with 50 questions that you

00:17:46.020 --> 00:17:51.010
need to answer so we know everything we need
to know about how you like your house to be.

00:17:51.010 --> 00:17:56.320
Like your temperature set point, and so on
so forth, and the result of the survey was

00:17:56.320 --> 00:18:00.769
that the immense majority of people said they
would never use a system like that. I just

00:18:00.769 --> 00:18:06.240
thought about my own home, there is the three
preferences for the thermostat set point, and

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:11.539
nobody can really agree on it, so that's an
example of you know trying to do that 50 times

00:18:11.539 --> 00:18:17.450
is impossible. We decided when we went to look
at nodes, we wanted to propose a project which

00:18:17.450 --> 00:18:25.130
was about building a real system that can
do such coordination, which addresses the scaling

00:18:25.130 --> 00:18:32.330
issue, and that means really addressing three
critical problems at the same time. One problem

00:18:32.330 --> 00:18:41.140
is whatever coordination mechanism you create,
its coordination is going to happen also through

00:18:41.140 --> 00:18:47.529
the power network. In our case here we focus
on the distribution network. The second is

00:18:47.529 --> 00:18:53.500
whatever mechanism you create needs to learn
and understand consumer preferences. In our

00:18:53.500 --> 00:19:01.540
case we picked the consumer to be homes initially.
The preferences of the homeowner have to be

00:19:01.540 --> 00:19:07.269
taken into account into the coordination. The
idea of picking up homes was also because

00:19:07.269 --> 00:19:13.539
each home by itself cannot really provide
much value back to the grid, but in coordination

00:19:13.539 --> 00:19:20.510
they can do a lot. And the third issue is that
whatever scheme you create, or architecture,

00:19:20.510 --> 00:19:28.370
or solution you create needs to be able to
account for the real constraints out there

00:19:28.370 --> 00:19:36.320
in terms of information exchange, so data exchange,
and communications, and so on. We spotted at

00:19:36.320 --> 00:19:43.120
the time this notion of, well we can use the
cloud to do coordination. And for example this

00:19:43.120 --> 00:19:48.210
is already done in data centers. So the cloud
runs the coordination of the communication

00:19:48.210 --> 00:19:54.929
between the server racks, but the key idea
even in the data center case is the cloud

00:19:54.929 --> 00:19:59.950
cannot do this in real time, because whenever
you put the cloud in a feedback loop there

00:19:59.950 --> 00:20:05.070
is delays on the information exchange, there's
reliability issues if you try to make it geographically

00:20:05.070 --> 00:20:11.580
spread, and this is all stuff we learned from
Google. So how do you do it so that the cloud

00:20:11.580 --> 00:20:19.010
is not really at every second and every minute
calculating and sending the decisions to all

00:20:19.010 --> 00:20:26.230
the homes. So that was this origin of this
project Powernet. Here's how the system that

00:20:26.230 --> 00:20:32.610
we came up with was laid out. And again we
started this project in kind of an unusual

00:20:32.610 --> 00:20:39.900
way because we decided to build a lab in Stanford,
put these devices and things together, work

00:20:39.900 --> 00:20:44.630
with Google, connect everything to the cloud,
etc, without knowing what the solution was

00:20:44.630 --> 00:20:49.750
going to be. And we experimented with tons
of algorithms until we figured something that

00:20:49.750 --> 00:20:54.420
worked and that also had some theoretical
justification, but that is still kind of work

00:20:54.420 --> 00:21:00.399
in progress, the theory around this. The key
idea of the system is that every home has

00:21:00.399 --> 00:21:10.450
a smart hub, a local intelligence, it's a computing
capability that communicates digitally with,

00:21:10.450 --> 00:21:16.130
you know, your inverters for the solar panel
storage, or EV chargers, and also with this

00:21:16.130 --> 00:21:23.600
smart dimmer. This is a new kind of smart panel
that was designed by our colleague professor

00:21:23.600 --> 00:21:28.929
Juan Rivas working with students. This panel
allows you to have high resolution measurements

00:21:28.929 --> 00:21:35.850
of all of the sub-circuits of the home, but
also the capability to do voltage control

00:21:35.850 --> 00:21:42.160
at the sub-circuit level. So you can have some
controllability of loads, appliances, and so

00:21:42.160 --> 00:21:48.049
on, even without actually the appliance being
smart necessarily. So that's kind of the resources

00:21:48.049 --> 00:21:52.950
you have, and normally this home would have
a bill, and it tries to minimize the bill using

00:21:52.950 --> 00:22:00.080
data and these resources. But in order to minimize
the bill and also to do coordination, which

00:22:00.080 --> 00:22:05.830
is homes might get together and say, well let's
shave the peak in this transformer because

00:22:05.830 --> 00:22:10.780
we'll get some compensation from the hotel
market, or maybe directly from the utilities,

00:22:10.780 --> 00:22:15.919
and from the demand response program. In order
to do all of that, they do need to understand

00:22:15.919 --> 00:22:22.470
and account for the impact they will have
on transformer capacities and network voltages.

00:22:22.470 --> 00:22:28.570
So that's kind of the setting, and how did
we solve this? I'm not going to enter into

00:22:28.570 --> 00:22:34.399
a lot of details but I'll go a little bit
into this project. First, it required thinking

00:22:34.399 --> 00:22:43.010
about this architecture, of how does the local
controllers and this kind of cloud coordinator

00:22:43.010 --> 00:22:48.780
engage? And we also imposed ourselves this
issue that we want to minimize the information

00:22:48.780 --> 00:22:54.590
exchange and the cloud cannot run in real
time. And so what we found is an architecture

00:22:54.590 --> 00:23:01.970
that has a planning stage that's run every
24 hours in alignment with the day ahead market,

00:23:01.970 --> 00:23:08.891
and in that planning stage it sends these
dynamic power bounds, so it's like establishing

00:23:08.891 --> 00:23:17.900
a bandwidth for each one of the homes in terms
of each hour, how much power are they allowed

00:23:17.900 --> 00:23:25.260
to draw or inject back into the grid. So instead
of having a static bound given by the transformer

00:23:25.260 --> 00:23:29.049
they can have a dynamic bound. And it turns
out, if you do these calculations cleverly

00:23:29.049 --> 00:23:34.899
this is enough to really protect the network
while enabling the coordination. We designed

00:23:34.899 --> 00:23:42.860
this smart dimmer, we also did lots of algorithms
with data from predictions to learning the

00:23:42.860 --> 00:23:48.110
customer preferences from observing their
choices and using appliances, and then the

00:23:48.110 --> 00:23:54.160
biggest effort in the project was a massive
kind of deployment. So we had a large-scale

00:23:54.160 --> 00:24:00.789
lab that was kind of built between Stanford
and slack, then we have deployments in a real

00:24:00.789 --> 00:24:09.011
world farm and 20 homes in Fremont where we
put you know battery storage, solar, we read

00:24:09.011 --> 00:24:15.550
their data, we kind of do all kinds of things.
I'm just gonna point out one thing here

00:24:15.550 --> 00:24:21.809
and then move on. You know, the critical challenge
on the coordination is that there is a spatial

00:24:21.809 --> 00:24:28.059
and temporal data asymmetry. It has not to
do just with the technical issues, it's because

00:24:28.059 --> 00:24:33.590
who knows what information? The utility knows
the network, but doesn't necessarily have access

00:24:33.590 --> 00:24:40.929
to behind the meter devices. DR providers have
access to their own devices, but no information

00:24:40.929 --> 00:24:46.860
on the overall load or the utility network,
and so on. So how do you deal with this data

00:24:46.860 --> 00:24:55.110
asymmetry? And so the design of Powernet addresses
this issue by figuring out how the information

00:24:55.110 --> 00:25:02.889
needs to be shared, and how that shared information
is utilized so that we can maximize the benefits

00:25:02.889 --> 00:25:09.640
for the consumers, their actual participants
in these systems. We initially -- when we designed

00:25:09.640 --> 00:25:15.740
this system one of the things that we learned,
and I think this is kind of the first major

00:25:15.740 --> 00:25:23.159
lesson, is seeking this kind of elusive optimality
that you see when you design algorithms and

00:25:23.159 --> 00:25:31.320
you test them in simulations. It's actually
detrimental in designing real scalable solutions.

00:25:31.320 --> 00:25:36.460
What we found is by sacrificing some of that
optimality, you can really take into account

00:25:36.460 --> 00:25:41.080
all of these practical constraints and achieve
good results. So you can reduce the voltage

00:25:41.080 --> 00:25:47.179
deviations that would result from people synchronizing
all their decisions. You can also do things

00:25:47.179 --> 00:25:54.320
like offer ramping services, regulation services,
we showed all of those. And then if you look

00:25:54.320 --> 00:26:01.269
at the end of the month into their bill, you
know you still achieve like 90 percent of

00:26:01.269 --> 00:26:06.980
the ideal oracle reduction, so they can't even
achieve that in practice, but just that small

00:26:06.980 --> 00:26:12.860
sacrifice results in big benefits. Here's kind of the
lab that we built and you can see this is

00:26:12.860 --> 00:26:17.929
a lab that was a partnership between industry,
the Stanford school of engineering and ARPA-E.

00:26:17.929 --> 00:26:24.990
So in industry the engagement was through
this Bits and Watts program that Liang directs.

00:26:24.990 --> 00:26:29.440
And you can see here we have like a smart
home with real appliances, it's connected to

00:26:29.440 --> 00:26:35.440
a panel, it's connected to a solar panel, to
a battery system, water heater, the whole thing,

00:26:35.440 --> 00:26:40.679
and to an EV charger. This home is connected
to a home that's a little bit more virtual,

00:26:40.679 --> 00:26:47.419
and here's like a grid emulator. All of this
system is connected to something like 10,000

00:26:47.419 --> 00:26:54.110
virtual homes in gridlab D and open DSS now.
This whole lab is connected to a similar lab

00:26:54.110 --> 00:27:00.419
in slack that had more of larger loads, and
were meant to emulate small and medium businesses.

00:27:00.419 --> 00:27:05.940
And this is where we initially tried to design
our solutions and demonstrate them, and we

00:27:05.940 --> 00:27:12.590
learned so much from first of all, interoperability.
Yes we do have the Sunspec alliance. But if

00:27:12.590 --> 00:27:18.960
you try to go and read any information from
real inverters, it doesn't really work. We did

00:27:18.960 --> 00:27:25.290
the field tests, and we have successfully deployed
this system in a farm, and then now we are

00:27:25.290 --> 00:27:31.830
doing it in homes in Fremont. You can see here
the cooling of cows in this real dairy facility

00:27:31.830 --> 00:27:37.750
is one of the largest dairy facilities in
the united states, and they spend about $200,000

00:27:37.750 --> 00:27:43.340
every summer with this cooling. And what
we are able to show is that using Powernet

00:27:43.340 --> 00:27:50.090
you can reduce about 50% of that cost. And what
Powernet does is it coordinates storage, the

00:27:50.090 --> 00:27:55.450
solar panels on the rooftop of the barns, and
the fan control, and tries to maintain the

00:27:55.450 --> 00:28:01.019
cow temperatures appropriately right now using
airflow measurements, but we have installed

00:28:01.019 --> 00:28:06.820
like a thermal camera. So as you can see in
the system I use this as an example because

00:28:06.820 --> 00:28:14.029
there's a lot of embedding of IOT solutions
from different vendors and so on. I'm just

00:28:14.029 --> 00:28:21.081
going to wrap up with our latest project. So
after we did Powernet, we realized there are

00:28:21.081 --> 00:28:28.090
a lot of principles on how you should design
the software solution for this and the architecture.

00:28:28.090 --> 00:28:33.100
And we wanted to look into how to do this
at an even larger scale. And we partnered up

00:28:33.100 --> 00:28:38.029
with folks in computer systems design here
at Stanford, and decided to look into this

00:28:38.029 --> 00:28:43.490
issue of virtualization, modularity, and layering,
which is their principles of how they do computer

00:28:43.490 --> 00:28:50.580
systems design. And that gave source to this
project called trustDER. The goal is to ensure

00:28:50.580 --> 00:28:57.570
resource virtualization, security ,and privacy
for all of these algorithms by virtualization.

00:28:57.570 --> 00:29:03.120
It's a simple concept: can you take one resource
and make it look like many virtual resources,

00:29:03.120 --> 00:29:08.059
or many resources and looking as one from
a software perspective, so then you write your

00:29:08.059 --> 00:29:12.559
solutions you don't have to worry about this.
And then in the privacy and fairness it's

00:29:12.559 --> 00:29:17.610
all about really protecting the privacy of
the customer. If I have to have data privacy

00:29:17.610 --> 00:29:23.009
how do I design algorithms? It turns out to
be pretty difficult to do that. To conclude,

00:29:23.009 --> 00:29:28.139
I just wanted to leave the form of engagement
we have had through all these projects is

00:29:28.139 --> 00:29:33.409
using this affiliate model for partnerships
through the Bits and Watts program. We work

00:29:33.409 --> 00:29:38.110
very closely with companies, gathering their
inputs, having them participate in board of

00:29:38.110 --> 00:29:44.740
advisors, and in some cases directly join our
research teams, and students who finish these

00:29:44.740 --> 00:29:48.899
projects sometimes go on to develop these
technologies commercially, sometimes they go

00:29:48.899 --> 00:29:55.269
into these companies as employees, and so on
so forth. I also have a flagship program that

00:29:55.269 --> 00:30:01.110
was started through this partnership around
EVs. Thank you very much. 
[ANITA]: Thank you Ram for

00:30:01.110 --> 00:30:06.390
sharing with us some of your work. We have
a few questions that were submitted. The first

00:30:06.390 --> 00:30:12.710
question is high level, then we'll dig a little
deeper. To start, would you take a few minutes

00:30:12.710 --> 00:30:21.840
to explain how DER measures and grid digitalization
affect grid resilience? 
[RAM]: In terms of the resiliency,

00:30:21.840 --> 00:30:29.929
I think there is two key ideas. One is understanding
how do you translate consumer flexibility

00:30:29.929 --> 00:30:36.610
into resiliency, because what is resiliency?
How do you measure it? For example, if I have

00:30:36.610 --> 00:30:42.210
an earthquake in San Mateo, it will affect
the homes. It might disrupt some of the solar

00:30:42.210 --> 00:30:50.389
PV, it might affect part of the grid. What do
I mean by being resilient? Do I have to provide

00:30:50.389 --> 00:30:55.610
full power to the remaining homes, or is it
part power, does it depend on the intensity

00:30:55.610 --> 00:31:01.990
of the event? We collaborated with the faculty
who does earthquake engineering to try to

00:31:01.990 --> 00:31:08.590
define these metrics around the ERs. And it
turns out there's two key aspects in my mind.

00:31:08.590 --> 00:31:15.330
One is you need to understand something like
what is the full load potential, half load

00:31:15.330 --> 00:31:22.259
potential, no load potential of each customer.
The second one I think it's it's a lot harder,

00:31:22.259 --> 00:31:28.759
which is really, any system to be resilient
beyond its individual capability, there is

00:31:28.759 --> 00:31:34.860
this notion of a networked resiliency. And
that means you need to understand how to model

00:31:34.860 --> 00:31:39.889
the switching in the distribution systems,
and adding those switches, as well as how does

00:31:39.889 --> 00:31:46.730
damage due to different events affect those
systems. I think it's a fairly open question.

00:31:46.730 --> 00:31:51.970
From a broader utility perspective I think
there is yet another question. Once you have

00:31:51.970 --> 00:31:58.809
a resiliency model you need to know what resources
are out there today in terms of DERs. From solar

00:31:58.809 --> 00:32:05.799
panels to different equipment, and the mapping
of this has been lagging tremendously. This

00:32:05.799 --> 00:32:12.019
data is hard to access, often unavailable. In
Stanford which started a project called the

00:32:12.019 --> 00:32:17.570
Stanford energy atlas that uses satellite
imagery, we work with people in machine learning

00:32:17.570 --> 00:32:23.139
and so on, and do country-wide mapping of these
resources. But there's many such efforts now

00:32:23.139 --> 00:32:29.830
that are happening, and I think without that
it's impossible to do this resiliency assessments.

00:32:29.830 --> 00:32:36.929
[ANITA]: Excellent, thank you. For our next question,
would you talk about time resolution of cloud

00:32:36.929 --> 00:32:42.830
coordination? What is time resolution, then
would you go deeper into that? Also, could you

00:32:42.830 --> 00:32:48.769
talk about the optimization model and how
you deal with changing household preferences?

00:32:48.769 --> 00:32:54.509
[RAM]: Overall, the idea is in the cloud coordinator
itself. We run it five to 15 minutes, so when

00:32:54.509 --> 00:33:00.519
you're offering ramping services, etc, but locally
we run it between a minute and a second. We

00:33:00.519 --> 00:33:05.259
have tried all of those combinations; the minute
seems to be more than enough. The optimization

00:33:05.259 --> 00:33:10.779
we started with STP Conic optimization to
take into account the network obviously required

00:33:10.779 --> 00:33:16.090
a lot of magic to make it work with like tens
of thousands of homes and large feeders, but

00:33:16.090 --> 00:33:23.860
recently we moved to the idea of successive
linearization, which despite the absence of

00:33:23.860 --> 00:33:29.590
strict performance guarantees, in all of our
practical examples and real feeder systems

00:33:29.590 --> 00:33:35.799
that we got from a few utility partners it
has done a really amazing job, and it's super

00:33:35.799 --> 00:33:43.659
fast. 
[ANITA]: Super, and I appreciate that insight. Next,
would you talk about what is the potential

00:33:43.659 --> 00:33:50.029
for developing local energy markets, distribution
operational markets as distinct from wholesale

00:33:50.029 --> 00:33:57.509
market opportunities, and how those distinctive
opportunities can be optimized? 
[RAM]: If you look

00:33:57.509 --> 00:34:04.820
not just today, but if you're looking at the
same grid 10 years from now, for example, you'll

00:34:04.820 --> 00:34:10.649
have much deeper penetration of EVs, you know
particularly kind of delivery vehicles

00:34:10.649 --> 00:34:15.919
and things like that that pull a lot of power,
and you will need to start managing the impact

00:34:15.919 --> 00:34:20.409
of this power on the distribution network
much more intelligently than today. Today there

00:34:20.409 --> 00:34:26.540
is a big slack capacity on the distribution
system so we are taking advantage of that.

00:34:26.540 --> 00:34:32.000
If you go beyond the united states and look
at other countries for example in India, or

00:34:32.000 --> 00:34:38.490
in Brazil, distribution transformers are often
already overloaded, and adding chargers is

00:34:38.490 --> 00:34:45.669
kind of a difficult thing because there is
no benefit for the flexibility that is monetized.

00:34:45.669 --> 00:34:51.359
[ANITA]: Great! And for our final question, there is
an interest in understanding what role universities

00:34:51.359 --> 00:34:57.170
play in the pipeline to invent and develop
new technologies, approaches, and solutions.

00:34:57.170 --> 00:35:02.880
And then to add to that, what needs do you
have to take the work that you and your teams

00:35:02.880 --> 00:35:09.309
have been doing from research and initial
concepts to commercialization and adoption?

00:35:09.309 --> 00:35:15.400
[RAM]: I think that first and foremost the mission
of the university is education, and I think

00:35:15.400 --> 00:35:23.211
a gigantic role is preparing students who
can go and address these challenges in the

00:35:23.211 --> 00:35:28.840
real world for all the companies, and policy
making, and so on out there. There's kind of

00:35:28.840 --> 00:35:35.059
the set of knowledge, the knowledge base they
need to have to address these problems in

00:35:35.059 --> 00:35:40.540
future energy systems is very different from
the past. You're not designing, you know, transmission

00:35:40.540 --> 00:35:46.780
lines anymore, you're talking about IOT, and
communications, and distributed algorithms

00:35:46.780 --> 00:35:53.480
and things like that, but also policy, economics,
social sciences. So I think that is kind of

00:35:53.480 --> 00:35:58.750
a first, and I really found a lot of success
with the students at Stanford and at other

00:35:58.750 --> 00:36:05.170
institutions. The second I think point that
is very critical is the university can serve

00:36:05.170 --> 00:36:13.020
almost like as a neutral zone where all these
different stakeholders can meet and exchange

00:36:13.020 --> 00:36:19.520
ideas, exchange information, exchange data, and
work together on problems. It's like a neutral

00:36:19.520 --> 00:36:25.730
ground because you know the mission is education,
and is not for profit organization. With that

00:36:25.730 --> 00:36:31.380
mindset, we have done a lot of the projects
that you see here. It came out of conversations

00:36:31.380 --> 00:36:37.160
with the industry with very close interactions
with them but with typically with companies

00:36:37.160 --> 00:36:43.520
that don't talk to each other, and then also
policy makers and so on. And another example

00:36:43.520 --> 00:36:48.990
of that is at Stanford, we have hosted lots
of interesting data sets where we collaborate

00:36:48.990 --> 00:36:54.010
in teams so that the owner of the data set
is aware of all that's going on, but that has

00:36:54.010 --> 00:37:01.040
led to really useful tools, like Wisdom is
an example of that, so shared data. And I think

00:37:01.040 --> 00:37:07.570
that the last piece, and this is something
that I think we need to improve from the universities,

00:37:07.570 --> 00:37:14.550
is really the awareness around these problems.
I think in the US today, you see young people

00:37:14.550 --> 00:37:20.680
much more in tune with the notions and ideas
around sustainability, wanting a 100% renewable

00:37:20.680 --> 00:37:27.420
grid, but the a deeper understanding of all
of the actual issues, the scale of what this

00:37:27.420 --> 00:37:33.069
transformation means, and then motivating people
to go and study this, get involved, and work

00:37:33.069 --> 00:37:37.849
in this industry, I think that's something
universities can do certainly a better job

00:37:37.849 --> 00:37:46.180
of. I think this commercialization side is
often misunderstood because I think the source

00:37:46.180 --> 00:37:52.250
of a lot of the interesting ideas is out of
thinking about, you know, the fundamentals. And

00:37:52.250 --> 00:37:58.680
out of that it manifests itself sometimes
as software, sometimes as just the people. And

00:37:58.680 --> 00:38:03.280
somebody asked about, you know, what's kind
of the challenge to commercialization. Having

00:38:03.280 --> 00:38:09.160
done startups that came out of Stanford in
other areas and comparing them to energy, I

00:38:09.160 --> 00:38:15.670
think there is two really important challenges.
One is energy systems are very critical,

00:38:15.670 --> 00:38:20.480
and today they are extremely reliable. So the
time scales at which you need to do a startup

00:38:20.480 --> 00:38:26.490
are very different. And this actually offers
an opportunity for new types of partnerships

00:38:26.490 --> 00:38:34.040
where companies can identify interesting technologies
developed in Stanford or Dartmouth, and then

00:38:34.040 --> 00:38:39.760
co-invest, and with the university and there's
many different models that are being explored

00:38:39.760 --> 00:38:45.410
to kind of bridge that time scale. There's
also programs like the one in Berkeley the

00:38:45.410 --> 00:38:51.960
cyclotron road that helps you do that launch.
I think the second challenge -- and this I think

00:38:51.960 --> 00:39:01.240
it's much more key -- as a community in grid,
we don't really do an excellent job of keeping

00:39:01.240 --> 00:39:08.720
what are kind of important practical problems,
that if addressed, there will be a ton of interest.

00:39:08.720 --> 00:39:15.240
If you go and you're working with medical
data, or you're thinking about data centers,

00:39:15.240 --> 00:39:20.550
each one of these communities has it very
clear, what are some like target problems that

00:39:20.550 --> 00:39:27.250
can be worked on by companies, researchers,
and so on. 
[ANITA]: Thank you, Ram, for this very informative

